# TLS and reverse proxy. That is the entire job.
#
# Caddy is here for one reason (ADR-0001 §2.4): automatic HTTPS with zero
# config. There is no certbot, no renewal cron, no `--deploy-hook`, no
# `fullchain.pem` path to get wrong — Caddy obtains and renews the certificate
# itself and reloads without dropping a connection. Do not add any of that back;
# the moving parts it removes are the whole reason it was chosen over nginx for a
# solo maintainer.
#
# Two things must exist in the environment before this file means anything, and
# `docker-compose.yml` refuses to start without them: DOMAIN and ACME_EMAIL.
# DNS for DOMAIN must already point at this box — the ACME HTTP-01 challenge is
# an inbound request to port 80 for a name the CA resolves itself, so an
# unpropagated record is a certificate that never issues.
{
	# Let's Encrypt sends expiry and policy notices here. Blank means the first
	# you hear about a problem is a browser warning.
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}

	# The admin API stays on its default, localhost:2019 INSIDE the container —
	# it is never published and never crosses the compose network. `caddy`'s
	# healthcheck in docker-compose.yml probes it, because it answers only once a
	# config has been loaded and applied, which is the difference between "the
	# process started" and "the Caddyfile parsed".
}

# ── shared by both origins ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The console and the student app are two ORIGINS, not two servers. ADR-0002
# decision 3 requires each client to be same-origin with the API it calls — that
# is what lets the refresh cookie be `SameSite=Strict` and why there is no CORS
# middleware — and it requires the student surface to be separate from the admin
# one. Two site blocks satisfy both.
#
# A second Caddy container would satisfy neither better. Origin separation is
# what the browser enforces, and it is already complete here; a second TLS
# terminator on the same box would double the certificate machinery and the
# memory for nothing. ADR-0001 §6 is emphatic about not adding infrastructure
# that buys nothing, and this would buy nothing.
#
# Snippets rather than arguments: Caddy supports `import name arg` with
# `{args[0]}`, but this file cannot be validated in CI (no daemon, and the
# `caddy` binary is not in the build), so it uses only syntax that has been
# stable since Caddy 2.0. Twelve duplicated lines of static-file serving are
# cheaper than a config that fails to parse on a box you cannot reach.
(shared) {
	# The exam payload is 150–250 KB of JSON per test and 200 clients pull one
	# each inside the same lobby window — the 320 Mbps burst in ADR-0001 §5.6,
	# and the only genuinely spiky thing in this system. Compressing it is the
	# cheapest thing on this page.
	#
	# The `match` is explicit rather than left to Caddy's default so it is
	# readable here: audio is already Opus and images are already compressed, and
	# re-compressing an 11 MB listening section spends CPU on this box to make
	# the file very slightly larger.
	encode {
		zstd
		gzip
		match {
			header Content-Type text/*
			header Content-Type application/json*
			header Content-Type application/problem+json*
			header Content-Type application/javascript*
			header Content-Type image/svg+xml*
		}
	}

	# A backstop ABOVE every limit the application enforces, not below one. The
	# largest body any handler accepts is the 32 MB DOCX import
	# (`authoring.py::MAX_IMPORT_BYTES`); media uploads are 5 MB parts with an
	# 8 MB ceiling (`object_storage.py::MAX_PART_BYTES`). A proxy limit under an
	# app limit replaces a message an author can act on — "An import may not
	# exceed 32 MB" — with an opaque 413 from a server they have never heard of.
	request_body {
		max_size 40MB
	}

	header {
		# One year, because the certificate renews itself and there is no
		# scenario in which this service should be reached over plaintext. Set
		# only after HTTPS is confirmed working: a browser that has seen this
		# header will refuse http:// for a year and cannot be told otherwise.
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		# Version-bearing banner. Free to remove, and it is one fewer thing
		# telling a scanner exactly which advisories to try.
		-Server
	}

	# Caddy already sets X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-Host,
	# and gunicorn is told to trust this container's fixed address on the compose
	# network (`--forwarded-allow-ips` in docker-compose.yml). That pairing is
	# what makes `request.client.host` the real client — which is what the
	# per-IP rate limits key on and what the copyright attestation records.
	#
	# WebSocket upgrades pass through untouched; reverse_proxy handles them
	# natively, so the realtime gateway at `/realtime` needs no configuration
	# here. Note it is NOT under `/api/v1` — that prefix carries the HTTP
	# contract and `scripts/check_api_coverage.py` compares it against
	# `openapi/openapi.yaml`. This block proxies every path, so both are served.
	#
	# Responses stream by default, which `MEDIA_DELIVERY=proxy` depends on: an
	# 11 MB listening section with Range support must not be buffered whole
	# before the first byte reaches the phone.
	# ── what belongs to the API ───────────────────────────────────────────────
	#
	# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and evaluated in order, so this is a
	# routing table rather than a pile of overlapping matchers. Everything the
	# application serves is enumerated here and everything else is the console.
	#
	# Getting this list SHORT is the mistake. It is not just `/api`:
	#
	#   /api/*     the contract, all 151 operations.
	#   /realtime  the WebSocket gateway. Deliberately NOT under /api/v1 —
	#              `check_api_coverage.py` compares that prefix against the
	#              OpenAPI document and a socket is not an HTTP operation.
	#   /internal/storage/*  **how listening audio reaches a student.** With
	#              `STORAGE_BACKEND=file` the presigned URLs the app mints point
	#              here. Serve this from the static root and every exam with audio
	#              breaks while the console looks perfectly healthy — which is why
	#              it is named first in the commit that added this block.
	#   /healthz, /metrics/*  operational, out of the contract on purpose.
	#
	# `handle` not `handle_path`: the application expects the full path, and
	# stripping the prefix would 404 every route.
	@backend path /api/* /realtime /realtime/* /internal/* /healthz /metrics/*
	handle @backend {
		# Caddy already sets X-Forwarded-For/Proto/Host, and gunicorn is told to
		# trust this container's fixed address (`--forwarded-allow-ips` in
		# docker-compose.yml). That pairing is what makes `request.client.host`
		# the real client — which the per-IP rate limits key on and the copyright
		# attestation records.
		#
		# WebSocket upgrades pass through untouched; reverse_proxy handles them
		# natively. Responses stream by default, which `MEDIA_DELIVERY=proxy`
		# depends on: an 11 MB listening section with Range support must not be
		# buffered whole before the first byte reaches the phone.
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}

}

# ── the SPA shell, identical for both apps ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Vite writes content-hashed filenames, so an asset that exists may be cached for
# a year; `index.html` must NEVER be, or a deploy leaves browsers loading an old
# bundle that references assets which no longer exist. That failure looks like a
# blank page nobody can reproduce, because their own browser has the new copy.
#
# `@shell` is matched as "not an asset" rather than `header /index.html`, which
# was the first attempt and is wrong: that matches only the LITERAL path, and the
# shell is almost never requested by name. It is served by `try_files` under `/`,
# `/tests`, `/versions/<xid>` — none of which the literal matcher sees.
(spa) {
	@hashed path /assets/*
	header @hashed Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	@shell not path /assets/*
	header @shell Cache-Control "no-store"

	# Fonts are self-hosted and content-stable (`web/public/fonts`). Same
	# reasoning as a hashed asset: the filename changes when the font does.
	@fonts path /fonts/*
	header @fonts Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	try_files {path} /index.html
	file_server
}

{$DOMAIN} {
	import shared

	# ── everything else is the admin console ─────────────────────────────────
	handle {
		root * /srv/web
		import spa
	}

	# JSON to stdout, where Docker's json-file driver picks it up and rotates it
	# (docker-compose.yml caps this at 5 × 10 MB). The application logs its own
	# request line with a trace id; this one is the edge's view — TLS version,
	# bytes, and the requests that never reached the app at all.
	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}
}

# ── the student app ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Its own origin, per ADR-0002 decision 3. A student's session and an admin's
# never share a cookie jar, a `localStorage`, or a service worker scope, and
# neither app can script the other — the browser enforces all of that from the
# hostname alone.
#
# **This needs its own DNS record.** `app.<domain>` must have an A record
# pointing at this box before the first `docker compose up`, or Caddy will fail
# to obtain a certificate for it and this block will not serve. The console is
# unaffected either way: certificates are per-site.
#
# Left as a subdomain rather than moving the console off the apex, because that
# would be a breaking change for anyone already bookmarked. If students should
# have the bare domain — a reasonable call, they are the volume — swap the two
# hostnames here and nothing else in this file changes.
app.{$DOMAIN} {
	import shared

	handle {
		root * /srv/student
		import spa
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format json
	}
}

# NOT here, deliberately: rate limiting. The ADR's topology sketch puts it at
# this layer, but Caddy has no rate limiter in the standard build — adding one
# means compiling a custom Caddy with a third-party module, which is exactly the
# "certbot-shaped complexity" this choice exists to avoid. The limits that matter
# are per-user and per-phone as much as per-IP (ADR-0001 §5.2: OTP is capped per
# phone AND per IP), which an edge proxy cannot see. They live in
# `app/api/limits.py` and `app/platform/ratelimit.py`, backed by Redis, applied
# to every route by one dependency in `app/api/main.py`.
